<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">62.47</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">62.85</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">42.20</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">38.80</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">57.70</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">59.12</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">75.19</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.17.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.17.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">25.31</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T5.11.1.18" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Mamba2</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">6.96B</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">8.62</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">40.02</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">39.59</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">69.78</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">69.68</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">42.20</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">42.20</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">65.19</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">63.77</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.12" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">76.66</span></td>
<td id="A3.T5.11.1.18.13" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="A3.T5.11.1.18.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">26.61</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Additional Results</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A3.T5" title="In Training Setup ‣ C.1 Language Modeling ‣ Appendix C Details on language modeling results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we expand upon the results illustrated in <a href="#S5.T2" title="In Matching Transformers Without Attention ‣ 5.2 Language modeling performance ‣ 5 Results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, providing the downstream tasks evaluation scores across all model scales considered (400M, 1B, 2.9B, and 7B). Overall, the performance observed is consistent across different parameter counts: while Mamba2 still achieves the lowest perplexity, ParaGRU and ParaLSTM remain competitive, with all three recurrent architectures often outperforming the Transformer. Importantly, our RNN cells show consistent improvement with scale, and the relative performance gaps between architectures remain stable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Synthetic tasks</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the synthetic tasks in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Language modeling performance ‣ 5 Results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we trained a <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single layer</span> of ParaGRU, ParaLSTM, Mamba2, and Transformer across a variety of synthetic tasks that have been used in the literature to evaluate the expressivity of sequential models. A summary of key results is shown in <a href="#S5.T1" title="In The Importance of Nonlinearities ‣ 5.2 Language modeling performance ‣ 5 Results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and full results are shown in <a href="#A3.T6" title="In Additional Results ‣ C.2 Synthetic tasks ‣ Appendix C Details on language modeling results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
The evaluation tasks considered are:</p>
<dl id="A3.I2" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="A3.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keep-n<sup id="A3.I2.ix1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">th</sup></span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A3.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input sequence <math id="A3.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x_{l}]_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x_{l}]_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> of values from a dictionary <math id="A3.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{l}\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{l}\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, output its <math id="A3.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th element, <math id="A3.I2.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A3.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I2.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parity</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A3.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input sequence of values in <math id="A3.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, output <math id="A3.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mod(\sum_{l=1}^{L}x_{l},2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>mod</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mod(\sum_{l=1}^{L}x_{l},2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A3.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A3.I2.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multiple-Query Associative Recall (MQAR)</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A3.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input sequence of key-value pairs <math id="A3.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,v)\in K\times V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,v)\in K\times V</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by noise interwoven with the keys, output for each key its associated value.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A3.I2.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A3.I2.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A3.I2.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-hops</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A3.I2.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input sequence of values from a dictionary <math id="A3.I2.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, for each element in the sequence “hop back” and report the value following its previous occurrence in the sequence. Keep on hopping back <math id="A3.I2.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times. The special case of 1-hop is also known as <em id="A3.I2.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">induction heads</em>.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models Description</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all models we train a single layer. The models under test are structured as: embedding layer with size matching the task vocabulary size, RMSNorm normalization layer, attention block / SSM / RNN cell, RMSNorm normalization layer, and a linear layer with size matching the task vocabulary size. We set the model dimension across all models to 64, and for Mamba2, ParaGRU, and ParaLSTM the hidden state dimension to 64. All models are configured with 4 heads for the attention, SSM and RNN cell respectively, and for models that use convolutional layers (Mamba2, ParaGRU and ParaLSTM for MQAR and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-hop) the convolutional kernel size is set to 4.
For Keep-n<sup id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">th</sup> and Parity described below, the SSM and RNN cell contain <span id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only</span> the mixer (in the tables we highlight this with <sup id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>), that is we remove all convolutional layers, normalization layers, and gating mechanisms. We do this to test the expressivity of the mixer themselves which is the main difference between the models. On the other hand, for MQAR and k-hops we use the full model (as described in <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Language Modeling ‣ Appendix C Details on language modeling results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a> and <a href="#A3.F9" title="In Models Description ‣ C.1 Language Modeling ‣ Appendix C Details on language modeling results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) since neither Mamba2 nor ParaGRU nor ParaLSTM are able to solve the task without these additional components. Moreover, for Keep-n<sup id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup">th</sup> we add positional encoding to Mamba2, ParaGRU, and ParaLSTM, since it is not possible to solve the task without it (the Transformer includes positional encoding in all tasks).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Setup</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For every task, we randomly obtain 10k samples as the training set and 100k samples as the test set (which we report on). For all tasks and models the sequence length is fixed to <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=100</annotation></semantics></math>. We train each architecture for up to 3,000 epochs with a batch size of 16. For optimization, we employ an AdamW optimizer (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>), cosine-scheduled learning rate starting at <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-}4</annotation></semantics></math>, and weight decay of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>. Tasks are trained on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU, across three different seeds (we report on the best result).
The norm of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a}_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a}_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{c}_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{c}_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in ParaGRU and ParaLSTM is clipped to a maximum of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.90</annotation></semantics></math>, except for Parity, where we found that norm clipping was detrimental to the cells’ ability to solve the task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Additional Results</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A3.T6" title="In Additional Results ‣ C.2 Synthetic tasks ‣ Appendix C Details on language modeling results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> expands upon the results in <a href="#S5.T1" title="In The Importance of Nonlinearities ‣ 5.2 Language modeling performance ‣ 5 Results ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, by including the Keep-n<sup id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">th</sup> task and k-hop for <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Overall, the observations from the main text are confirmed: nonlinear RNNs can be more effective at solving specific tasks, where the inclusion of more complex recurrence formulations aids solution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A3.T6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="A3.T6.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Single-layer accuracy on synthetic tasks. Reporting best accuracy of 3 runs. <math id="A3.T6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="()^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">()^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes results computed on the recurrent cell only. We indicate the number of parameters for each model in parentheses.</span></figcaption>
<div id="A3.T6.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:60.1pt;vertical-align:-28.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-37.8pt,6.6pt) scale(0.820396381665422,0.820396381665422) ;">
<table id="A3.T6.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A3.T6.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T6.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A3.T6.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Model</span></td>
<td id="A3.T6.8.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A3.T6.8.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Keep-n</span><sup id="A3.T6.8.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A3.T6.8.1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">th</span></sup><sup id="A3.T6.8.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A3.T6.8.1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">†</span></sup></td>
<td id="A3.T6.8.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A3.T6.8.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Parity</span><sup id="A3.T6.8.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A3.T6.8.1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">†</span></sup></td>
