<p id="A4.SS3.p3.6" class="ltx_p">This is the minimum implementation the user must provide to be able to apply the RNN cell. Just with this, the user can already toggle between sequential and parallel application<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Notice that the recurrence_step method is designed to work regardless of whether the input is batched or not, and whether it is a full sequence or a single element: this allows to reuse the exact same method for the different application modes of the RNN cell.</span></span></span> by selecting model.mode=SEQUENTIAL or model.mode=PARALLEL. Moreover, since the ParaGRU cell specified above presents a diagonal structure, the user can directly leverage the efficient implementations provided in RNNCellDiagImpl for Jacobians assembly (via autograd) and manipulation, and the corresponding CUDA-accelerated solver is also available via selecting model.mode=PARALLEL_CUDA. Still, by manually providing the formula for the Jacobians <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Cell Jacobians ‣ 3 Adapting GRU and LSTM for ParaRNN ‣ ParaRNN: Unlocking Parallel Training ofNonlinear RNNs for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, one can boost performance by avoiding the reliance on autograd:</p>
<div id="A4.SS3.p3.7" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_python ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
